{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-1ea58f83-2792-4f39-9f7f-b2489382554e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c227f9836b25ecd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8001-2pydiqur/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8001-E004.md","content":"# Track component team compliance and enforce the September 15 deadline\n\n## Description\n\nRun the strategy's core coordination mandate: aggregate every component team's compliance status via tracking tickets linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, drive teams to completion, and enforce the September 15, 2026 deadline so that zero RHOAI component images are left unaccounted for — every image has either been rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base image or has a filed, acknowledged OCP exception with a remediation plan. This epic delivers the aggregated compliance view for OCP 5.0 visibility and the close-out audit.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Ensure every component team has a tracking ticket linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, providing the aggregated compliance view (per-image accounting comes from the PQC Readiness Matrix).\n- Send weekly reminders to teams with outstanding switch or exception work; escalate to leadership (per the Mara/Devin sync decision) for teams at risk of missing the deadline without a filed exception.\n- Verify that OCP exception filings include the component's TLS stack, the reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, and a documented remediation plan.\n- Close out by September 15, 2026: audit that zero unaccounted-for images remain — each image is either rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base or covered by a filed, acknowledged exception.\n- Verification of switched images: inside any switched container, `update-crypto-policies --show` reports `DEFAULT:PQ` and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` shows ML-KEM-512/768/1024, measured by an automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload. Note: TLS-scanner CI tooling is owned by the separate Central TLS Profile strategy (RHAISTRAT-7816); this epic consumes, and does not build, that scan infrastructure. On FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — that is expected behavior, not a compliance failure. This criterion applies to OpenSSL-backed components only.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- All component team tracking tickets are linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, providing an aggregated compliance view for OCP 5.0 (AC-3).\n- By September 15, 2026: every RHOAI component image has either (a) been rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base image, or (b) a filed and acknowledged OCP exception in OCPEXCEPT with a documented remediation plan including the component's TLS stack and reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM — zero unaccounted-for images remain (AC-2).\n- For OpenSSL-backed switched images, the crypto-policy verification (`DEFAULT:PQ`, ML-KEM kem algorithms listed) is confirmed across the RHOAI 3.6 payload (AC-1, consuming CI scan tooling owned by RHAISTRAT-7816).\n- Weekly reminder cadence and leadership escalation for at-risk teams are demonstrably exercised between now and the deadline.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 [P0]**: verifies and enforces that all RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image (or are covered per below).\n- **HLR-2 [P0]**: enforces the exception path — teams unable to comply by September 15 file an OCP exception with a remediation plan.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":63166,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b14d6098c05a58eb","agent_id":"a8bd95be9a2e5f76c","session_id":"12e54bbc-7543-4961-a6e8-72b51d0a133c","uuid":"bf4f257c-826e-41df-a278-c0d4cb59cb7c","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:48:54.920Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
