{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e04b613eb36ff03","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E003\n3\ttitle: Author the PQC Readiness Matrix per component\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: AI Core Platform Security\n6\tteam: RHOAI PM\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8001-E001\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8001-E001\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If PQC variants or the RHEL 9.8 rebase are not confirmed available\n15\t    in time, the matrix rows for those image groups change from base image switch\n16\t    to OCP exception path\n17\tai_signals:\n18\t  change_specificity: 1\n19\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n20\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n21\t  existing_foundation: 1\n22\t  open_questions: 0\n23\t  external_dependency: 0\n24\t  human_process_gates: -1\n25\t  repo_access: 0\n26\t  architecture_claims: 1\n27\timplementation_type: null\n28\tinvestigation_signals: null\n29\tai_implementability: High\n30\tai_implementability_score: 3\n31\tjira_key: null\n32\tbranch: null\n33\t---\n34\t# Author the PQC Readiness Matrix per component\n35\t\n36\t## Description\n37\t\n38\tCreate the strategy's planned design doc — the **PQC Readiness Matrix** — cataloging every RHOAI component image (per the RHOAI 3.5 CSV relatedImages inventory and the organized local image inventory) with its current base image, target PQC base image, TLS stack, and compliance path (base image switch vs. OCP exception). This matrix is the per-image accounting artifact that compliance tracking and deadline enforcement build on, and it records the resolution of the PQC base availability unknowns for the python/nodejs-based and RHEL9-ELS-based image groups.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- Enumerate all RHOAI component images from the RHOAI-Build-Config rhoai-3.5 CSV (~153 relatedImages) and the locally maintained organized image inventory.\n43\t- For each image, record: current base image, target PQC base (per the strategy's base image mapping), TLS stack (system OpenSSL / rustls+ring / Java-NSS / Go crypto), and compliance path.\n44\t- Record compliance paths resolved from the confirmed availability of `ubi9-minimal-pqc` and `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, and from the resolved availability of the python/nodejs PQC variants and the RHEL 9.8 rebase.\n45\t- Record known non-OpenSSL TLS stacks from architecture context: fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring — cannot inherit PQC from a base image swap), TrustyAI explainability service (Java/NSS), and the trainer data cache (Rust TLS posture per audit spike RHOAIENG-7196).\n46\t- Document that the switch is final-runtime-stage-only per image (no builder image change).\n47\t- Mark images whose teams are expected to file OCP exceptions, with the reason the component cannot negotiate ML-KEM.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- Every image in the RHOAI 3.5 payload inventory has a row with current base, target base or exception path, TLS stack, and owning team.\n52\t- Each row's compliance path is consistent with the confirmed PQC base availability and rebase timing determinations.\n53\t- Rows for fms-guardrails-orchestrator, TrustyAI, and the trainer data cache reflect their non-OpenSSL TLS stacks and the exception/remediation path where applicable.\n54\t- The matrix documents that only the final runtime stage changes per image (HLR-3).\n55\t- The matrix is technically reviewed against the architecture context files (fms-guardrails-orchestrator.md, PLATFORM.md) and by engineering review.\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability\n58\t\n59\t- **HLR-1 [P0]**: the matrix is the per-image plan for reaching PQC-enabled bases.\n60\t- **HLR-2 [P0]**: the matrix identifies which images are expected to take the OCP exception path and why.\n61\t- **HLR-3 [P0]**: the matrix records the final-stage-only constraint per image.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac1ecaaf3df4b37e","agent_id":"a3b5f159801f9c1af","session_id":"12e54bbc-7543-4961-a6e8-72b51d0a133c","uuid":"e417ee89-f5a7-4e31-97cc-de0f13a8c2fd","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:53:14.315Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e04b613eb36ff03","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
