{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-51fce20c-1a40-4193-9e86-8f2e9f5ab217","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaafd0f1608e1b34","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8001-2pydiqur/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-8001-decomp-review.md","content":"# RHAISTRAT-8001 Decomposition Review\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 10/14 — fail\nRecommendation: revise\n\nTwo criteria scored 0 (DAG Coherence, Type Correctness), triggering the auto-fail rule regardless of the total score. The decomposition is otherwise strong: HLR coverage, boundaries, signal scoring, ACs, and completeness are all solid, and the conditional-branch design and tiered-delivery reasoning are well constructed.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\nAll three P0 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E006, E004), HLR-2 (BRANCH-B-E006, E004), HLR-3 (E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E006). HLR-4 (ML-DSA) is explicitly deferred with justification matching the strategy's out-of-scope list. The traceability matrix is present and accurate. Priority inheritance is correct throughout the main plan (E001 P0 → E003 P0 → E004 P0; E005 P1 has no dependents). No priority collapse — all mapped HLRs are P0 and the deferred HLR is kept out. The delegation of per-team switch/exception execution to component teams (Scope Notes) is faithful to the strategy's own text and does not create a coverage gap, since E003/E004 provide the per-image accounting and enforcement.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\nDiagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: every edge in the `graph TD` diagram (dependency → dependent direction, correct convention) has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and vice versa; no edges reference nonexistent epics; no cycles. No konflux-onboarding or repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are N/A. Branch files both set `branch` and `gated_by`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies`.\n\nHowever, there is a **critical** missing edge discoverable from epic content: both branch epics update the PQC Readiness Matrix — BRANCH-A-E006 scope/AC: \"Update the readiness matrix records for the switched image groups\" / \"Readiness matrix records for the switched groups are updated\"; BRANCH-B-E006 scope/AC: \"update the readiness matrix records for exception-path images\" / \"Readiness matrix records for exception-path images are updated\". The readiness matrix is the artifact produced by E003. Neither branch epic has E003 in `dependencies` or an edge in the DAG, so as drawn, BRANCH-A-E006 (gated only by E001 and E002) can start and complete before E003 creates the matrix it is required to update. Add `E003 → BRANCH-A-E006` and `E003 → BRANCH-B-E006` edges (in both the diagram and the branch epics' `dependencies`). Since this is a missing data/artifact dependency edge, this criterion scores 0.\n\nThe E002 → BRANCH-A-E006 edge (same build-configuration surface) and the deliberate absence of E001 → E002 (tiered delivery) are both justified. Main-plan critical path of 3 is acceptable given the gating investigation, despite the S effort estimate — the multi-team scope justifies it.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nEach epic has a coherent single team/component tuple (E001 AIPCC, E002/BRANCH-A DevOps/Build, E003/E004/BRANCH-B RHOAI PM, E005 Performance team). E003 and E004 share a team but are genuinely different logical work (authoring an accounting artifact vs. running tracking/enforcement) — correctly separate epics. No epic is oversized relative to ~2 weeks. The readiness matrix is single-sourced in E003 (branch epics only update rows), so there is no scope duplication. Minor observation, not scored: E004 and BRANCH-B-E006 both touch exception-filing tracking (aggregate enforcement vs. targeted coordination for variant-blocked images); the split is defensible but the handoff between them could be stated more explicitly.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\nTwo major issues:\n\n1. **E004's `gated_by` target is not in its direct `dependencies`.** E004 sets `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8001-E001` but its `dependencies` list contains only `RHAISTRAT-8001-E003` — E001 is reachable only transitively (E004 → E003 → E001). Automated pipeline consumers detect gates from the `dependencies` list, so this gate is invisible to them. Either add E001 to E004's `dependencies` (and diagram) or remove the `gated_by` — E004's rewrite is already fully mediated by E003's rewrite, so the latter is arguably correct.\n2. **E005 is an Investigation with no downstream dependents.** The decomposition's own DAG justification states the performance threshold \"does not change which epics exist or what they do — the switch and coordination work proceeds the same way; the threshold defines ship-gating pass/fail,\" and no epic depends on E005. By the rubric test, an \"Investigation\" whose outcome changes no downstream structure should be an Implementation (it produces concrete deliverables: a signed-off numeric threshold and a measurement report with pass/fail determination) or an acceptance criterion elsewhere. The work content is well-scoped either way; the type is wrong.\n\nAll other gating metadata is correct: E003, BRANCH-A-E006, and BRANCH-B-E006 each have `gated_by` present in direct `dependencies` with fully populated `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach), and the `remove`/`rewrite` actions match the actual failure semantics.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nEvery epic carries the signal set matching its type (`investigation_signals` for E001/E005, `ai_signals` for the five Implementation epics), and an ai-signals rationale file exists for all seven epics. Frontmatter values match the rationale tables in every case. Spot-checks hold up: E001 `source_accessibility: 0` is correct (the gating answer — committed AIPCC dates — is not yet on the record, with `human_judgment_required: -2` carrying the human-decision aspect); E002 `external_dependency: +1` is justified (both base images confirmed available); E003 `human_process_gates: -1` matches the pending Architecture Review; E004 `external_dependency: -1` / `human_process_gates: -1` match its dependence on teams and human-process work; BRANCH-A `external_dependency: -1` matches its dependence on AIPCC-delivered variants. No scoring errors found.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nAll seven epics have testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria and scope (e.g., E004 carries AC-1's crypto-policy verification with the FIPS-mode and OpenSSL-only caveats preserved, AC-2's zero-unaccounted-images audit, AC-3's ticket linking; E001 carries the escalation trigger; E005 carries the Sprint-2 sign-off deadline). No rule-mandated ACs apply: there are no replacement epics (no rollback/feature-flag AC needed), no konflux chain, and no docs-authoring epic is required since the strategy's Documentation Support gate is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\". E003, the doc-like epic, appropriately carries a technical-review AC anyway. No epic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID — all cross-references use full IDs or artifact names.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nStrategy scope is fully accounted for: base image switch coordination (E002, BRANCH-A), exception path (BRANCH-B, E004), tracking-ticket aggregation and deadline enforcement (E004), readiness matrix design doc (E003), variant/rebase investigation (E001), and the performance NFR (E005). Strategy-level context is carried forward well — the FIPS-mode caveat, RHAISTRAT-7816 tooling boundary, Rust TLS NFR contradiction, trainer data cache unknown, and pending process gates are all recorded in Health Warnings and reflected in epic scopes/ACs. Conditional branches cover both bounded outcomes and partial outcomes. One minor issue: TLS-stack-blocked components (fms-guardrails-orchestrator, Trusty