{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab4c95ea0baf22da","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tscore: 10\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Missing edges E003 -> BRANCH-A-E006 and E003 -> BRANCH-B-E006: both\n10\t    branch epics scope/ACs update readiness matrix records produced by E003, but neither\n11\t    lists E003 in dependencies and the DAG diagram has no such edge, so a branch epic\n12\t    can complete before the matrix it must update exists. Add the edges to the diagram\n13\t    and both branch epics dependencies lists.'\n14\t- severity: major\n15\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n16\t  description: E004 sets gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 but its dependencies list contains\n17\t    only RHAISTRAT-8001-E003, so the gate target is reachable only transitively; automated\n18\t    pipeline consumers detect gates from the dependencies list. Add E001 to E004 dependencies\n19\t    or drop the gated_by (the rewrite is already mediated by E003).\n20\t- severity: major\n21\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n22\t  description: E005 is typed Investigation but has no downstream dependents, and the\n23\t    decomposition itself states its outcome does not change which epics exist or what\n24\t    they do. Per the rubric test it should be an Implementation (it produces a signed-off\n25\t    threshold and a measurement report) or an acceptance criterion on the compliance\n26\t    work.\n27\t- severity: minor\n28\t  criterion: Completeness\n29\t  description: TLS-stack-blocked components (fms-guardrails-orchestrator, TrustyAI)\n30\t    must file OCP exceptions in both branch outcomes, but the coordination work for\n31\t    them lives only inside BRANCH-B-E006, which is removed if all variants are confirmed;\n32\t    in a branch-A outcome it is covered only implicitly by E004. Move the unconditional\n33\t    TLS-stack exception coordination out of the branch.\n34\terror: null\n35\t---\n36\t# RHAISTRAT-8001 Decomposition Review\n37\t\n38\t## Review Summary\n39\t\n40\tScore: 10/14 — fail\n41\tRecommendation: revise\n42\t\n43\tTwo criteria scored 0 (DAG Coherence, Type Correctness), triggering the auto-fail rule regardless of the total score. The decomposition is otherwise strong: HLR coverage, boundaries, signal scoring, ACs, and completeness are all solid, and the conditional-branch design and tiered-delivery reasoning are well constructed.\n44\t\n45\t## Criterion Details\n46\t\n47\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n48\tAll three P0 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 (E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E006, E004), HLR-2 (BRANCH-B-E006, E004), HLR-3 (E002, E003, BRANCH-A-E006). HLR-4 (ML-DSA) is explicitly deferred with justification matching the strategy's out-of-scope list. The traceability matrix is present and accurate. Priority inheritance is correct throughout the main plan (E001 P0 → E003 P0 → E004 P0; E005 P1 has no dependents). No priority collapse — all mapped HLRs are P0 and the deferred HLR is kept out. The delegation of per-team switch/exception execution to component teams (Scope Notes) is faithful to the strategy's own text and does not create a coverage gap, since E003/E004 provide the per-image accounting and enforcement.\n49\t\n50\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n51\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check passes: every edge in the `graph TD` diagram (dependency → dependent direction, correct convention) has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and vice versa; no edges reference nonexistent epics; no cycles. No konflux-onboarding or repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are N/A. Branch files both set `branch` and `gated_by`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies`.\n52\t\n53\tHowever, there is a **critical** missing edge discoverable from epic content: both branch epics update the PQC Readiness Matrix — BRANCH-A-E006 scope/AC: \"Update the readiness matrix records for the switched image groups\" / \"Readiness matrix records for the switched groups are updated\"; BRANCH-B-E006 scope/AC: \"update the readiness matrix records for exception-path images\" / \"Readiness matrix records for exception-path images are updated\". The readiness matrix is the artifact produced by E003. Neither branch epic has E003 in `dependencies` or an edge in the DAG, so as drawn, BRANCH-A-E006 (gated only by E001 and E002) can start and complete before E003 creates the matrix it is required to update. Add `E003 → BRANCH-A-E006` and `E003 → BRANCH-B-E006` edges (in both the diagram and the branch epics' `dependencies`). Since this is a missing data/artifact dependency edge, this criterion scores 0.\n54\t\n55\tThe E002 → BRANCH-A-E006 edge (same build-configuration surface) and the deliberate absence of E001 → E002 (tiered delivery) are both justified. Main-plan critical path of 3 is acceptable given the gating investigation, despite the S effort estimate — the multi-team scope justifies it.\n56\t\n57\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n58\tEach epic has a coherent single team/component tuple (E001 AIPCC, E002/BRANCH-A DevOps/Build, E003/E004/BRANCH-B RHOAI PM, E005 Performance team). E003 and E004 share a team but are genuinely different logical work (authoring an accounting artifact vs. running tracking/enforcement) — correctly separate epics. No epic is oversized relative to ~2 weeks. The readiness matrix is single-sourced in E003 (branch epics only update rows), so there is no scope duplication. Minor observation, not scored: E004 and BRANCH-B-E006 both touch exception-filing tracking (aggregate enforcement vs. targeted coordination for variant-blocked images); the split is defensible but the handoff between them could be stated more explicitly.\n59\t\n60\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n61\tTwo major issues:\n62\t\n63\t1. **E004's `gated_by` target is not in its direct `dependencies`.** E004 sets `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8001-E001` but its `dependencies` list contains only `RHAISTRAT-8001-E003` — E001 is reachable only transitively (E004 → E003 → E001). Automated pipeline consumers detect gates from the `dependencies` list, so this gate is invisible to them. Either add E001 to E004's `dependencies` (and diagram) or remove the `gated_by` — E004's rewrite is already fully mediated by E003's rewrite, so the latter is arguably correct.\n64\t2. **E005 is an Investigation with no downstream dependents.** The decomposition's own DAG justification states the performance threshold \"does not change which epics exist or what they do — the switch and coordination work proceeds the same way; the threshold defines ship-gating pass/fail,\" and no epic depends on E005. By the rubric test, an \"Investigation\" whose outcome changes no downstream structure should be an Implementation (it produces concrete deliverables: a signed-off numeric threshold and a measurement report with pass/fail determination) or an acceptance criterion elsewhere. The work content is well-scoped either way; the type is wrong.\n65\t\n66\tAll other gating metadata is correct: E003, BRANCH-A-E006, and BRANCH-B-E006 each have `gated_by` present in direct `dependencies` with fully populated `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach), and the `remove`/`rewrite` actions match the actual failure semantics.\n67\t\n68\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n69\tEvery epic carries the signal set matching its type (`investigation_signals` for E001/E005, `ai_signals` for the five Implementation epics), and an ai-signals rationale file exists for all seven epics. Frontmatter values match the rationale tables in every case. Spot-checks hold up: E001 `source_accessibility: 0` is correct (the gating answer — committed AIPCC dates — is not yet on the record, with `human_judgment_required: -2` carrying the human-decision aspect); E002 `external_dependency: +1` is justified (both base images confirmed available); E003 `human_process_gates: -1` matches the pending Architecture Review; E004 `external_dependency: -1` / `human_process_gates: -1` match its dependence on teams and human-process work; BRANCH-A `external_dependency: -1` matches its dependence on AIPCC-delivered variants. No scoring errors found.\n70\t\n71\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n72\tAll seven epics have testable ACs derived from the strategy's acceptance criteria and scope (e.g., E004 carries AC-1's crypto-policy verification with the FIPS-mode and OpenSSL-only caveats preserved, AC-2's zero-unaccounted-images audit, AC-3's ticket linking; E001 carries the escalation trigger; E005 carries the Sprint-2 sign-off deadline). No rule-mandated ACs apply: there are no replacement epics (no rollback/feature-flag AC needed), no konflux chain, and no docs-authoring epic is required since the strategy's Documentation Support gate is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\". E003, the doc-like epic, appropriately carries a technical-review AC anyway. No epic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID — all cross-references use full IDs or artifact names.\n73\t\n74\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n75\tStrategy scope is fully accounted for: base image switch coordination (E002, BRANCH-A), exception path (BRANCH-B, E004), tracking-ticket aggregation and deadline enforcement (E004), readiness matrix design doc (E003), variant/rebase investigation (E001), and the performance NFR (E005). Strategy-level context is carried forward well — the FIPS-mode caveat, RHAISTRAT-7816 tooling boundary, Rust TLS NFR contradiction, trainer data cache unknown, and pending process gates are all recorded in Health Warnings and reflected in epic scopes/ACs. Conditional branches cover both bounded outcomes and partial outcomes. One minor issue: TLS-stack-blocked components (fms-guardrails-orchestrator, TrustyAI) must file exceptions in **both** branch outcomes, yet the coordination work for them (directing to the OCPEXCEPT template, tracking filings to acknowledgment) lives only inside BRANCH-B-E006, which is removed if all variants are confirmed; in that world it is covered only implicitly by E004's generic reminders and enforcement. Consider moving the unconditional TLS-stack exception coordination out of the branch (e.g., into E004) so it does not disappear with Branch B.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b2646fd9a047d87","agent_id":"a379f05c2d4fd22a0","session_id":"12e54bbc-7543-4961-a6e8-72b51d0a133c","uuid":"dd5e93bf-aea0-436f-b85c-8837f7c76371","timestamp":"2026-10-22T11:58:27.281Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab4c95ea0baf22da","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
