{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f151f67430c010f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-8001 Decomposition — Base Image Compliance: Post-Quantum Cryptography Support\n11\t\n12\t## Strategy Summary\n13\t\n14\tRHAISTRAT-8001 is a **compliance coordination tracker** for switching all RHOAI runtime images to PQC-enabled base images by **September 15, 2026** (RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0), or filing an OCP exception via OCPEXCEPT. Two PQC base images are confirmed available (`ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`); PQC variants of the python-311/312 and nodejs-22 bases are unconfirmed (RHOAIENG-7195 / AIPCC), and the AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase (AIPCC-7258) gates RHEL9-ELS image adoption.\n15\t\n16\t## Scope Notes (read before reviewing epic coverage)\n17\t\n18\t- **Per-team switch and exception-filing work is deliberately NOT decomposed into per-team epics.** The strategy explicitly delegates it: \"Implementation work is owned by each component team\", \"OCP exception filing for individual teams — each team files their own\", and the mechanism is \"teams link their tracking tickets to RHAISTRAT-8001\". HLR-1/HLR-2 coverage is therefore achieved through the coordination, readiness-matrix, pipeline-enablement, and deadline-enforcement epics below — not through ~150 per-team epics the strategy provides no team→image mapping for.\n19\t- **Not triaged below-threshold** (Step 0): although the effort estimate is S, the strategy affects all RHOAI component images across multiple teams (fails single-component/single-team), and it contains genuine unknowns (unconfirmed PQC base variants, RHEL 9.8 rebase timing) whose answers change which downstream work exists.\n20\t- **Not docs-only** (Step 0): affected components require code/config/pipeline changes (owned by teams), plus coordination deliverables owned here.\n21\t- Go ML-KEM, TLSAdherence, and TLS-scanner CI are owned by RHAISTRAT-7816 (out of scope). ML-DSA certificates are deferred (HLR-4, not mapped).\n22\t\n23\t## Epic List (main plan)\n24\t\n25\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n26\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 | Confirm PQC base image variant availability and RHEL 9.8 rebase timing with AIPCC | Investigation | AIPCC team | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E002 | Enable confirmed PQC base images in the RHOAI build pipeline | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E003 | Author the PQC Readiness Matrix per component | Implementation | RHOAI PM | P0 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E004 | Track component team compliance and enforce the September 15 deadline | Implementation | RHOAI PM | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E005 | Define the PQC TLS handshake performance regression threshold and validate latency | Implementation | Performance team | P1 |\n32\t\n33\t### Conditional branch epics (gated by E001 outcome; not standalone Jira issues)\n34\t\n35\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Branch |\n36\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|--------|\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-BRANCH-A-E006 | Integrate confirmed PQC variant base images into the build pipeline and coordinate team switches | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 | A — variants/rebase confirmed in time |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-BRANCH-B-E006 | Coordinate OCP exception filings for images without available PQC base images | Implementation | RHOAI PM | P0 | B — variants/rebase not available in time |\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: PQC variant + rebase availability\"]\n45\t    E002[\"E002 Pipeline: confirmed PQC bases\"]\n46\t    E003[\"E003 PQC Readiness Matrix\"]\n47\t    E004[\"E004 Compliance tracking + deadline enforcement\"]\n48\t    E005[\"E005 Implementation: perf threshold + validation\"]\n49\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E006 Variant switch path (branch A)\"]\n50\t    BB[\"BRANCH-B-E006 Exception path (branch B)\"]\n51\t\n52\t    E001 --> E003\n53\t    E003 --> E004\n54\t    E001 --> E004\n55\t    E001 --> BA\n56\t    E002 --> BA\n57\t    E003 --> BA\n58\t    E001 --> BB\n59\t    E003 --> BB\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\tMain-plan critical path: **E001 → E003 → E004** (length 3). Branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n63\t\n64\t## DAG Justification\n65\t\n66\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n67\t|------|------|-----------|\n68\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The readiness matrix's per-image remediation path (switch vs. exception) for ~45 python/nodejs-based and RHEL9-ELS-based images is determined by the investigation outcome. True gate: `rewrite` — matrix content changes, epic exists either way. |\n69\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (artifact consumer) | Deadline enforcement and the \"zero unaccounted images\" close-out audit consume the readiness matrix as the per-image accounting artifact. |\n70\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (gated work, direct edge) | E004's `gated_by` target: its close-out verification content (rebuilt images vs. filed OCP exceptions) is determined by the investigation outcome. Kept as a direct edge (not only transitively via E003) so automated consumers detect the gate from the `dependencies` list. |\n71\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E006 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Branch A only exists if PQC variants and the rebase are confirmed in time (bounded outcome, ≤3 possibilities → conditional branch). |\n72\t| E002 → BRANCH-A-E006 | Rule 13 / epic boundary (same surface) | Branch A builds on the build-pipeline base-image wiring for the same build configuration surface; serial to avoid two epics editing the same pipeline configuration independently. |\n73\t| E003 → BRANCH-A-E006 | Rule 12/13 (artifact producer → consumer) | Branch A updates readiness matrix records for the switched image groups — the matrix must exist before those updates can be made. |\n74\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Branch B only exists if variants/rebase are not available in time; affected teams are directed to the OCP exception path. |\n75\t| E003 → BRANCH-B-E006 | Rule 12/13 (artifact producer → consumer) | Branch B updates readiness matrix records for exception-path images — the matrix must exist before those updates can be made. |\n76\t| No edge E001 → E002 | Rule 17 / Step 5.5 (tiered delivery) | The two confirmed base images (`ubi9-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`) enable Tier 1 partial value now; pipeline enablement for them does not wait on the investigation. |\n77\t| No edge E005 → any epic | Rule 4 / Step 6.5 | The performance threshold does not change which epics exist or what they do — the switch and coordination work proceeds the same way; the threshold defines ship-gating pass/fail. Typed Implementation: the epic produces concrete deliverables (a signed-off numeric threshold and a measurement report with a pass/fail determination) and no downstream epic depends on it; runs in parallel. |\n78\t| No konflux/repo-onboarding edges | Rules 5–10 | No new component or repository is provisioned by this strategy; all work happens in existing repos, Jira, and existing build infrastructure. N/A. |\n79\t\n80\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n81\t\n82\t| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n83\t|-----|----------|-------|\n84\t| HLR-1: All RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image | P0 | E002 (pipeline enablement for confirmed bases), E003 (matrix records per-image target base), BRANCH-A-E006 (switch path for variant-gated images), E004 (compliance verification and close-out). Per-team switch execution is delegated to component teams via linked tracking tickets (see Scope Notes). |\n85\t| HLR-2: Teams unable to comply file an OCP exception by September 15 with remediation plan | P0 | BRANCH-B-E006 (exception-filing coordination for variant/rebase-blocked images), E004 (zero-unaccounted-images enforcement, plus unconditional exception-filing coordination for TLS-stack-blocked components — fms-guardrails-orchestrator, TrustyAI — in both branch outcomes). Per-team filing is delegated (see Scope Notes). |\n86\t| HLR-3: PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage changes, no builder image change | P0 | E002 (consumption guidance reflects final-stage-only switch), E003 (matrix documents the final-stage-only change per component), BRANCH-A-E006 (acceptance criterion). |\n87\t| HLR-4: PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | Not mapped — explicitly deferred to a follow-on phase (strategy out-of-scope). |\n88\t\n89\t## Conditional Branches\n90\t\n91\tE001 has two bounded outcomes that change downstream structure:\n92\t\n93\t- **Branch A — PQC variants and/or RHEL 9.8 rebase confirmed available before September 15, 2026**: `RHAISTRAT-8001-BRANCH-A-E006` — wire the confirmed variant base images into the build pipeline and coordinate affected teams' final-stage switches.\n94\t- **Branch B — variants/rebase not available in time**: `RHAISTRAT-8001-BRANCH-B-E006` — coordinate OCP exception filings (with TLS stack and remediation plan) for affected images.\n95\t\n96\tBoth branch epics set `branch`, `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8001-E001` (also in `dependencies`), and `gate_failure_impact` (`remove` — each branch epic does not exist if the other outcome holds). Both also depend on the readiness matrix epic (E003), since each updates readiness matrix records for its image groups. Partial outcomes (e.g., rebase lands but nodejs variant slips) are handled per image group: confirmed groups follow Branch A, unconfirmed groups follow Branch B; the fallback_approach text on each branch epic states this. `epic_count` counts the 5 main-plan epics; the 2 branch epics are additional.\n97\t\n98\t## Health Warnings\n99\t\n100\t1. **Scope trap — AC-1 measurement infrastructure**: AC-1 is \"measured by automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\", but TLS-scanner CI is owned by RHAISTRAT-7816 (out of scope). The verification is carried as an acceptance criterion on E004 with a cross-strategy dependency note; if RHAISTRAT-7816 does not deliver scan tooling, the measurement mechanism for AC-1 is unowned.\n101\t2. **Priority inversion risk — performance NFR**: The performance NFR is marked \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\", is ship-gating (\"must be resolved before Sprint 2 ends\", Performance Team Support: Required), yet no HLR carries it. It is modeled as a P1 Implementation epic (E005 — threshold definition, measurement, and pass/fail report) running parallel to the P0 coordination chain; if leadership treats it as ship-blocking, its effective priority exceeds its label.\n102\t3. **NFR vs. scope contradiction — Rust TLS migration**: The Security NFR requires fms-guardrails-orchestrator (and any pure-Rust TLS components) to \"support ML-KEM hybrid key exchange after migration\", but no Rust TLS migration is scheduled in this strategy's scope; architecture context confirms rustls+ring with no OpenSSL in the release image. Remediation ownership is delegated to component teams via OCP exception remediation plans (TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452). The NFR cannot be satisfied by this strategy's epics alone.\n103\t4. **Unresolved trainer data cache TLS posture**: \"Rust TLS posture unknown\" (audit spike RHOAIENG-7196). Tracked as an input/acceptance criterion on the readiness matrix (E003) rather than a new Investigation epic, because an existing spike (RHOAIENG-7196) already owns the question.\n104\t5. **Strategy-level process gates pending**: Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and Documentation Support are all \"Pending human review\" — these human gates are unresolved at the strategy level and may re-scope downstream work.\n105\t6. **Missing standard sections**: The strategy has no formal Affected Components table (component, change, owner) — components are described in prose (\"All RHOAI component images\") with the image inventory delegated to external links. Proceeded with available information.\n106\t\n107\t## Tiered Delivery\n108\t\n109\tExternal dependency (PQC python/nodejs base variants, AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase) has uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n110\t\n111\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**:\n112\t  - E001 — risk-reduction Investigation with AIPCC (escalation trigger: no committed date by early September).\n113\t  - E002 — build pipeline can consume the two confirmed PQC base images; the ~68 `ubi9-minimal`-based images can be switched by their teams now.\n114\t  - E005 — performance threshold definition and validation (independent of base variant availability).\n115\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated by E001)**:\n116\t  - E003, E004 — readiness matrix consolidation and deadline close-out (need resolved switch-vs-exception paths).\n117\t  - BRANCH-A-E006 / BRANCH-B-E006 — the conditional compliance path for python/nodejs-based (~11 images) and RHEL9-ELS-based (~34 images, gated on the rebase) images.\n118\t\n119\t## Component Mapping Notes (non-obvious)\n120\t\n121\tStrategy components are platform-wide (\"All RHOAI component images\"); canonical `.context/rhai-components.txt` names assigned per epic:\n122\t\n123\t| Strategy term | Canonical component |\n124\t|---------------|--------------------|\n125\t| AIPCC team (PQC base variant confirmation/delivery) | AIPCC Productization |\n126\t| DevOps/Build team (base image pipeline updates) | Build and Release |\n127\t| Platform-wide PQC compliance coordination, readiness matrix, deadline enforcement | AI Core Platform Security |\n128\t| Performance team (TLS handshake regression threshold/measurement) | PerfScale |\n129\t\n130\tSupporting architecture context used: `fms-guardrails-orchestrator.md` (rustls+ring, non-FIPS-validated, no OpenSSL in release image), `PLATFORM.md` (FIPS posture, component runtime summary), `trainer.md` (Go operator, cluster TLS security profile usage).\n131\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82463234dec7213e","agent_id":"a762836ed961a9ea0","session_id":"12e54bbc-7543-4961-a6e8-72b51d0a133c","uuid":"79a69812-46dd-4fe5-9621-43ff619c2a39","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:04:01.999Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f151f67430c010f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
