{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-930f43b5bfbe0d8b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E004\n3\ttitle: Track component team compliance and enforce the September 15 deadline\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: AI Core Platform Security\n6\tteam: RHOAI PM\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8001-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8001-E003\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8001-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If PQC variants or the RHEL 9.8 rebase are not confirmed available\n16\t    in time, close-out verification shifts from rebuilt images to filed OCP exceptions\n17\t    for those image groups\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 1\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: 0\n24\t  external_dependency: -1\n25\t  human_process_gates: -1\n26\t  repo_access: 0\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: Medium\n31\tai_implementability_score: 1\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Track component team compliance and enforce the September 15 deadline\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tRun the strategy's core coordination mandate: aggregate every component team's compliance status via tracking tickets linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, drive teams to completion, and enforce the September 15, 2026 deadline so that zero RHOAI component images are left unaccounted for — every image has either been rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base image or has a filed, acknowledged OCP exception with a remediation plan. This epic delivers the aggregated compliance view for OCP 5.0 visibility and the close-out audit.\n40\t\n41\t## Scope\n42\t\n43\t- Ensure every component team has a tracking ticket linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, providing the aggregated compliance view (per-image accounting comes from the PQC Readiness Matrix).\n44\t- Send weekly reminders to teams with outstanding switch or exception work; escalate to leadership (per the Mara/Devin sync decision) for teams at risk of missing the deadline without a filed exception.\n45\t- Coordinate exception filings for TLS-stack-blocked components — fms-guardrails-orchestrator (rustls+ring; TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452) and the TrustyAI explainability service (Java/NSS; JDK 29 timeline September 2027) — unconditionally, regardless of PQC base variant availability: direct each owning team to the OCPEXCEPT template and track their filings to acknowledgment.\n46\t- Verify that OCP exception filings include the component's TLS stack, the reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM, and a documented remediation plan.\n47\t- Close out by September 15, 2026: audit that zero unaccounted-for images remain — each image is either rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base or covered by a filed, acknowledged exception.\n48\t- Verification of switched images: inside any switched container, `update-crypto-policies --show` reports `DEFAULT:PQ` and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` shows ML-KEM-512/768/1024, measured by an automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload. Note: TLS-scanner CI tooling is owned by the separate Central TLS Profile strategy (RHAISTRAT-7816); this epic consumes, and does not build, that scan infrastructure. On FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — that is expected behavior, not a compliance failure. This criterion applies to OpenSSL-backed components only.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- All component team tracking tickets are linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, providing an aggregated compliance view for OCP 5.0 (AC-3).\n53\t- By September 15, 2026: every RHOAI component image has either (a) been rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base image, or (b) a filed and acknowledged OCP exception in OCPEXCEPT with a documented remediation plan including the component's TLS stack and reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM — zero unaccounted-for images remain (AC-2).\n54\t- For OpenSSL-backed switched images, the crypto-policy verification (`DEFAULT:PQ`, ML-KEM kem algorithms listed) is confirmed across the RHOAI 3.6 payload (AC-1, consuming CI scan tooling owned by RHAISTRAT-7816).\n55\t- Weekly reminder cadence and leadership escalation for at-risk teams are demonstrably exercised between now and the deadline.\n56\t- TLS-stack-blocked components (fms-guardrails-orchestrator, TrustyAI explainability service) have filed OCP exceptions via OCPEXCEPT with their TLS stack, the reason they cannot negotiate ML-KEM, and a documented remediation plan, tracked to acknowledgment — regardless of PQC base image variant availability.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- **HLR-1 [P0]**: verifies and enforces that all RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image (or are covered per below).\n61\t- **HLR-2 [P0]**: enforces the exception path — teams unable to comply by September 15 file an OCP exception with a remediation plan; owns the unconditional exception-filing coordination for TLS-stack-blocked components (fms-guardrails-orchestrator, TrustyAI) in both branch outcomes.\n62\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82463234dec7213e","agent_id":"a762836ed961a9ea0","session_id":"12e54bbc-7543-4961-a6e8-72b51d0a133c","uuid":"c7e8909b-867c-49a8-9c05-980afb7d3cdf","timestamp":"2026-10-22T12:04:13.396Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-930f43b5bfbe0d8b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
